Measuring success plays a central role in justifying and advocating for a statistical or
data science consulting or collaboration program (SDSP) within an academic institu
tion. We present several specific metrics to report to targeted audiences to tell the
story for success of a robust and sustainable program. While gathering such metrics
includes challenges, we discuss potential data sources and possible practices for
SDSPs to inform their own approaches. Emphasizing essential metrics for reporting,
we also share the metric gathering and reporting practices of two programs in greater
detail. New or existing SDSPs should evaluate their local environments and tailor
their practice to gathering, analysing and reporting success metrics accordingly. This
approach provides a strong foundation to use success metrics to tell compelling
stories about the SDSP and enhance program sustainability. The area of success met
rics provides ample opportunity for future research projects that leverage qualitative
methods and consider mechanisms for adapting to the changing landscape of data
science.
KEYWORDS
data science consulting, metrics, reporting, stakeholders, statistical collaboration
1 | INTRODUCTION
Statistics and data science are core disciplines with methodology that can be applied to a broad array of research collaborations across many disci
plines such as engineering, health sciences, life sciences, physical sciences and social sciences (ASA, 2018, Sharp et al., 2016). Many colleges and
universities have organized groups, departments, programs or research institutes that provide this kind of collaborative support. The structures
and service models can vary greatly in order to adapt to the possible structures and models that higher education institutions currently employ to
address their respective approaches to data science (e.g., Herndon, 2021; Huebner et al., 2024). Recent national developments highlight the
importance of data science across sectors and place increasing pressure on academic institutions to effectively engage in this landscape, which
requires proficiency with data (Vitale et al., 2022).
As data science is a newer term, much of the literature we review comes from statistical consulting and collaboration. A common theme
across the literature for statistical collaboration and consulting is that success metrics can help establish the value of such activities, but these
