may be difficult to measure. Sharp et al. (2016) present a survey on the perceived value of statistical consulting, while an American Statistical
Association Position Paper (ASA, 2018) discusses the difficulty consulting programs and individual consultants in academia face with assessment
because this work differs from more traditional disciplinary scholarship.
Part of this difficulty may arise from the variety of work that a program of this nature performs. Consulting topics are rarely routine, and col
laborative statisticians and data scientists may spend a large percentage of their time educating domain experts about recent developments in sta
tistics or various statistical pitfalls that may be an issue for a research study (Sauerbrei et al., 2014). While statistical or data science collaborations
help domain experts answer their questions and improve statistical practice (see Vance & Pruitt, 2022), without explicit acknowledgement, includ
ing co-authorship of publications or funding from grants, this work can be invisible to those not involved in creating the work. That invisibility is a
problem for collaborative statisticians and data scientists because universities often assign substantial value to authorship of scholarly works
when evaluating both individuals and programs (ASA, 2018).
Language and terminology used to describe data science and statistical consulting and collaboration depend heavily on the program
type and model. Many programs find value and recognition in emphasizing the collaborative nature of their work and prefer terminology
that reflects those goals instead of those around clients or customers. Programs based in academic libraries tend to identify as a service
with users or patrons of that service, consistent with common terminology in the field (Press, 2022). Other programs prefer to adopt termi
nology around consultation and the provision of expert advice. In this paper, we seek to identify metrics of success that apply across these
different programs and identities, recognizing that specific programs will and should apply the terminology most appropriate to their
model.
For the purposes of this paper, we use the term Statistics and Data Science Program (SDSP) to refer to any entity offering collaboration or
consulting in the areas of data science and statistics. This covers a broad array of programming, from departments within a university library to
a biostatistics or data science core, and many funding mechanisms, from cost recovery or fee based models to no-cost models for users. The
SDSP may reside in many different administrative units within an institution, for example, a college, an academic department, the library or the
office of research. SDSPs may employ different organizational, staffing and financial models, and SDSP leadership may refer to the people they
work with as collaborators, clients or users. In this article, we address entry-level reporting and success metrics that may apply across the dif
ferent models of SDSPs. Once good reporting practices for these metrics have been set up and as an SDSP becomes more established, more
complex and nuanced success metrics may be collected. The aim of this paper is to provide guidance for establishing a baseline level of
reporting in an SDSP.
Reporting activities and success metrics are crucial for evaluating and advocating for an SDSP for many reasons. These include analysing
use and performance, reporting to funders, advertising services or negotiating staffing levels. Tracking publications and grants is often stan
dard in academia. However, measuring the successes of SDSPs often requires quantifying multiple dimensions of work such as collaboration
and consulting activities, partnerships, education or staffing levels (e.g., Lee et al., 2012; Mani et al., 2021). Specific metrics are mentioned
consistently throughout the relevant literature, such as co-authorship on publications (Carter et al., 1986; Griffith et al., 2022; Parker
et al., 2021) and grants or other funding support (Griffith et al., 2022; Vance, 2015), but these do not address all dimensions of a
robust SDSP.
Appropriate metrics of success can help demonstrate value, attract partners, educate potential users and university leadership or identify
trends or risks. They provide transparency, show credibility and can build trust with SDSP staff and stakeholders. They can be used to
identify areas of need and to argue for promotion of personnel. Success metrics are more than just numbers. They illustrate that the SDSP delivers
what it promises and may facilitate engagement with stakeholders. Effective demonstration of success through reporting and metrics should also
help the SDSP work towards sustainability. As Herndon (2021) points out, all data science programs should consider the ways that they will
ensure the longer term plans for sustaining their work. Data science as a field provides particular challenges to medium and longer term planning,
as the field regularly shifts (Herndon, 2021).
The choice of success metrics depends in part on the strategic plan of the university and the SDSP itself (Huebner et al., 2024; Parker
et al., 2021). Building support and enthusiasm for the SDSP may require convincing arguments for resources that resonate with university leaders.
While it is important to recognize that success metrics can be both quantitative or qualitative measures, the scope of this paper focuses primarily
on quantitative metrics.
The purpose of this paper is to describe a variety of success metrics that address multiple dimensions of collaborative statistical and data sci
ence programs with the goal of evaluating and advocating for these programs, their personnel and other resource needs. Engaging ideas can be
relatable based on reason, ethical appeals, motives and attitudes (logos, ethos and pathos) (Meyerson, 2015). Storytelling and qualitative metrics
can be important aspects of obtaining support for an SDSP.
In Section 2, we outline potential goals and considerations for reporting success metrics, including external reporting, internal reporting and
program evaluation. In Section 3, we describe different metrics, data sources to gather such metrics, and discuss two examples. Section 4 high
lights privacy and ethical considerations related to metrics and reporting. In Section 5, we conclude with a discussion on modern reporting ideas,
future work and the changing landscape of data science.
