2 | REPORTING SUCCESS METRICS
SDSPs differ in their mission, administrative home and funding models and may engage with different partners (Huebner et al., 2024). The con
tent of any reporting will depend on the purpose and the audience. Reporting to stakeholders may involve different sets of information about
the SDSP and its activities, presented in varying levels of detail. For example, if an annual report is used to summarize the usage statistics for a
college sponsor, the leadership of that college might also appreciate a comparison of the number of cases across colleges. An internal report
prepared for leaders in the administrative home of the SDSP can be more extensive and detailed. Furthermore, a self-evaluation to determine
programmatic successes and areas for growth can be quite lengthy and detailed. Finally, decisions about personnel retention, promotion or pro
fessional development opportunities will rely on granular details. Well-planned and thoughtfully collected success metrics will frequently be
able to be used in reports for multiple audiences. Retrospective collection of these details can be more difficult than aggregating them for more
general reporting.
A key message is that there is no single reporting template that will work for all SDSPs. As noted above, the level of detail for reporting
success metrics depends on the preferences of the stakeholders and the types of data available. Ideally, a cycle of feedback between stake
holders and SDSPs can inform a program's efforts to adapt, update and ensure satisfaction. With changes in administrative leaders and strate
gic goals, this can be a continuous process. Keeping this flexibility in mind when planning what metrics to track is essential to facilitate agile
adaptation.
2.1 | External reporting
External reports of SDSP activities serve multiple purposes (Table 1). Success metrics can be used for advertising by showcasing the types of
activities the SDSP is engaged in and the reach and impact it can have for the research community of the university. The SDSP can use external
reports for justification of the funding it receives or budget requests for future years. Reports may also be needed to negotiate or renew agree
ments for staffing levels.
SDSPs should consider the formats for their reporting needs. Most programs will still need to prepare traditional, document-style reports for
a variety of their internal and external stakeholders. However, new formats may prove beneficial to consider depending on the audience and
need. Programs might consider developing a modular slide deck to tailor for presentations to various stakeholder audiences. Other modern modal
ities of reports include photos and video messages, which can support visual and auditory storytelling. Reports can be made interactive to high
light different aspects of a program and include links that open to more detailed sections depending on a reader's interest. Programs can leverage
internal expertise in data visualization, web design, videography and even animation to create engaging representations of their metrics.
