2.2 | Internal reporting
By contrast, internal reporting (Table 2) will likely be more granular than external reporting. SDSP leadership will want to be immersed in details
of the program. Regular evaluation, at least biannually, of the full program in the context of an SDSP's strategic goals and priorities is critical for
maintaining alignment and focus. Internal reporting may also be part of staff evaluations and used for identifying appropriate professional devel
opment opportunities. Additionally, internal reporting helps the SDSP review and evaluate its own achievements and successes.
One critical goal of internal reporting is to avoid stagnation. As the fields of statistics and data science evolve, so does the use of such tech
niques in collaborative or consulting relationships. Reviewing internal reports regularly can help SDSP leadership identify areas of growth and
areas of stagnation early enough to address them.
2.3 | Internal program evaluation
An SDSP may wish to assess metrics for the purpose of internal program evaluation and improvement outside of the reporting activities described
in the previous section. Analysis of the collected metrics can provide insight on attainment of internal goals, identify new areas to support or areas
to consider reducing and provide direction for skill development and hiring. A program may have a goal to reach broadly across campus and
assessment of usage by campus division or department would provide valuable insight on work towards that goal. If a program conducts a review
for the types of support requested, they may uncover new topics of demand or changes in requests over time that could inform staffing. Con
versely, the SDSP may find areas that were once robustly requested waning in demand. These changes can inform areas in which program mem
bers can develop their skills or the topics can be embedded in job advertisements to stay current with the shifting needs in data science and
statistical work. While many of these metrics intersect with the needs of internal and external reporting, the goals and approaches in assessment
may diverge. Program evaluation can also help identify areas to highlight in reporting or areas that may warrant some program iteration before
sharing more widely.
3 | IDENTIFYING SUCCESS METRICS AND DATA SOURCES
The overarching goal for any SDSP should be to collect success metrics in a way that is well organized and able to be easily analysed in the way
that best suits the needs of the SDSP. It is important to recognize that success metrics can be both quantitative or qualitative measures. Quantita
tive metrics can be pulled from existing university databases, captured in database systems of SDSPs or even collected via forms and surveys.
Qualitative metrics are less likely to exist in university databases but may include testimonials of experiences by both clients and data science con
sultants, case studies, brokering new research relationships between faculty from different units or even learning outcomes from workshops,
among many possibilities. SDSPs have many options for how to collect and store data and metrics about the program. The related data may be
housed in a self-hosted database system, a third-party application or in a series of spreadsheets in a shared drive. The importance of specific met
rics differs depending on the priorities and investments of different stakeholders.
Not all SDSPs are in the position to gather the metrics listed above. However, if such metrics would be desirable, then the SDSP can strive to
build a robust data collection system to inform writing of reports for different stakeholders. SDSPs currently use a variety of approaches to collect
these data, from using existing campus databases, to carefully designing their own databases, or using other data management solutions. A data
base provides an essential foundation for collecting, storing and maintaining the data necessary for day-to-day management of an SDSP and
reporting success metrics. Such databases may be quite small and simple for new SDSPs but may grow in size and complexity over time as the
SDSP encounters the need for new features and reporting capabilities. The scale at which a SDSP operates may drive some of those needs.
