It is important to remember that data are only useful if they are of high quality. For any kind of user input, the ease of contributing to data
fields is an important factor. For example, individual consultants may contribute their time records and brief case notes more accurately and
regularly if this is a web-based system rather than weekly Excel spreadsheets. A well-thought-out intake form is highly valuable if it has suffi
cient information about the project and future collaborator that can be filled out quickly (1–3 min). Longer or more complex intake forms dis
courage clients from completing them and may lead to incomplete or inaccurate responses. Structured data from closed-ended, categorical
questions is very useful for some purposes, but collecting some unstructured, open-ended text is often necessary for things like narrative pro
ject descriptions.
To present these ideas more clearly, we present two programs with different collection and reporting processes.
3.1 | Example 1: collecting and aggregating data sources for reporting at North Carolina State University
Data sources may come from numerous and varied systems that do not interact directly with each other. At North Carolina State
University (NCSU), for example, data science consulting takes place through the Data Science Academy (DSA) and the North Carolina State Uni
versity Libraries (Marchesoni et al., 2024). The Libraries use many systems to track user interactions across the organization. As a result, the Data
and Visualization Services (DVS) department of the Libraries gathers usage data from the following: a service email account; personal email
accounts from staff members; a university system for listing workshops; a Libraries reporting mechanism for reporting interactions with curricular
classes; a chat system; a Calendly appointment booking tool; and a home-grown help desk reporting system. Users of the consultation service are
also invited to complete an optional post-consultation feedback survey. The service email account is shared between the DVS and the DSA Con
sulting Program and implemented as a delegated institutional Gmail account. The staff and student data science consultants responding to consul
tation requests through the account leverage a detailed system of labelling to triage and track status, as well as gather information about the
consultation requests. A script is used to generate a dataset from that email account that includes the information from the assigned labels that is
analysed for program assessment and metric reporting (Marchesoni et al., 2023). The department works closely with the Head of Impact Analysis
for the Libraries to gather all of this data from the disparate systems. The data are gathered and then joined with affiliates' campus profile infor
mation with all identifying information removed. The data are then made available for analysis by DVS staff via a shared Google Drive as a collec
tion of spreadsheets (Peak & Ashmore, 2023). Staff in the department then use that data to conduct internal evaluations, populate annual reports
and fulfil other reporting needs. Summary metrics and reports without individually identifying information are shared with organizational or cam
pus leadership. Given the emphasis on interdisciplinarity at the institution and within the NC State University Libraries, DVS routinely reports
annual consulting usage by campus affiliate type (faculty, staff, undergraduate student, graduate student etc.); colleague affiliation; type of ques
tion; medium of consultation; and other metrics.
