3.2 | Example 2: building a data collection system at Michigan State University
Michigan State University's (MSU) Center for Statistical Training and Consulting (CSTAT) provides a nice example of more advanced and custom
ized reporting. CSTAT currently uses a custom-built SQL Server database and web application called CSTATAPP (Segur et al., 2023) to manage its
consulting operations. Clients can submit service requests directly into the database after logging in via either a standard or guest MSU user
account. The intake form collects data about the client, the project, type of help being requested, project deadline, associated grants (both pro
posals in preparation and grants that are already funded) and expected funding source for CSTAT services. The database allows CSTAT staff to
update the resulting case, user and grant records and link them to additional information. CSTATAPP can record case status, dates of key events
(case submission, first contact and case closure; start and end of consultant assignments; grant deadlines, submission and decision dates; etc.),
date-stamped case notes, time spent on the case, grant funding and more. Administrative staff can assign a case to one or more partnership agree
ments with service type indicators that define whether, and why, the case is within or outside the scope of a given agreement and what, if any,
usage limits apply. Features support consultants' ability to review case histories, handle their caseloads, manage their time and get follow-up pro
mpts about upcoming deadlines. CSTAT staff have role-based access to the database: Consultants can view their own projects and built-in
reports, while administrative users have broader access to view and edit data and can run additional built-in reports. Additional reporting capabili
ties are supported by a custom R package (Pierce & DeCicco, 2024) that interfaces with the database and uses Quarto (https://quarto.org) scripts
to generate more sophisticated, professionally formatted reports with information that can be used in weekly, monthly and annual reports.
4 | PRIVACY, ETHICS AND PROFESSIONAL STANDARDS
As they undertake this important work to demonstrate their impact, SDSPs must consider issues around ethics, user privacy and regulations in
gathering and reporting metrics. This holds particularly true for SDSPs based in libraries where professional codes of ethics and organizational pol
icies regulate the release of identifying information about individuals using library affiliated services. Professional associations provide directions
to libraries in regard to privacy. For example, the American Library Association (ALA) includes privacy as a core value, stipulating in the Library Bill
of Rights that privacy and confidentiality are a right of library users (ALA, 2006b; ALA, 2007). The Association of College and Research Libraries
(ACRL) interprets this Bill of Rights for the higher education setting and reinforces a strong commitment to privacy, stipulating ‘The privacy of
library users is and must be inviolable. Policies should be in place that maintain confidentiality of library borrowing records and of other informa
tion relating to personal use of library information and services (ALA, 2006a)’. ACRL further reinforces the professional commitment to privacy in
their guidance for assessment in academic libraries (ALA, 2017). Academic libraries commonly have local policies or statements that address the
local requirements for user privacy, such as the NC State Libraries Privacy Statement (NC State Libraries, 2019). In this context, identifying infor
mation should not be shared beyond the Libraries, including within other parts of the university.
SDSPs with an administrative home in academic libraries must consider how their assessment practices adhere to the professional values of
the field, as well as comply with local policy requirements. However, all SDSPs should carefully consider the ethical implications of their assess
ment practices and reporting approaches in regards to success metrics. The values and practices of user privacy and confidentiality practiced in
academic libraries can serve as a model to support all SDSPs, regardless of administrative placement within a university or institution. Adherence
to individual privacy need not trade off with reporting the metrics that underscore the impact of the SDSP. The program can report summary sta
tistics showcasing the depth and breadth of their work without releasing individuals' names or other identifying information.
In addition to professional ethics, those working on success metrics should consider any Institutional Review Board (IRB) requirements for
their metrics collection, analysis and reporting. Data collected about usage of SDSPs offerings include information about humans, which may be
subject to IRB oversight. The IRB will typically determine that this type of work for the purposes of internal program assessment and improve
ment is not subject to review. However, SDSPs wishing to conduct some types of studies on their own programs or to include any data derived
from human information in scholarly output such as a presentation or publication may be considered as engaging in research and therefore would
be subject to further review by their local IRB. SDSPs should work closely with representatives from their IRB to ensure appropriate and ethical
treatment of any humans studied in the course of their assessment and reporting. Programs working with students in the United States may need
to consider if the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA) applies to collected metrics and likewise ensure compliance with federal law
pertaining to data about students. SDSPs are responsible for ensuring adherence to any other applicable rules and regulations.
5 | DISCUSSION
Regardless of the organizational model that they employ, SDSPs provide valuable expertise to their campus to improve data science and statistical
work at the organization. Programs should collect metrics that highlight this impact in support of their reporting needs and to facilitate internal
evaluation. This paper outlines suggested metrics categorized and clustered by purpose to include in reporting of success, as well as suggests
