potential data sources. Gathering metrics from various sources and distilling them into meaningful measures requires careful planning and consid
eration, but this work is essential to demonstrate the impact of the work and ensure its longer-term sustainability.
Those working in SDSPs may naturally gravitate towards focusing more on quantitative summaries and trends. Indeed, this paper focused on
some essential, high level and primarily quantitative metrics all SDSPs should consider for measuring their success. As programs gain proficiency
with their basic needs for metrics and reporting it is interesting to consider other types of evaluation. For example, exploring qualitative metrics
and mixed methods approaches can deepen and humanize some of the quantitative measures. While storytelling is not qualitative data analysis,
case studies and examples may bring wider exposure of research successes of collaborators and showcase applications of data science, and the
types of questions SDSP consultants helped solve. Testimonials of satisfied collaborators are appreciated and can be encouraging to stakeholders
and future users alike. Growth areas of a SDSP may be identified by gathering input from across the university or informed by developments in
professional organizations and societies.
As the assessment matures for individual SDSPs and for this area of data science consulting and collaboration as a field, programs need to
continuously update their methods of evaluation. Just as we evaluate our programs for their effectiveness and impact, we need to keep our
assessment practices current and relevant to address evolving needs. Continued maturation opens up new and deeper areas to explore through
effective assessment. As described above, investigation of deeper, qualitative studies on SDSP success represents an interesting area for further
development. The authors of this paper suggest that a significant future area that warrants further research is the exploration of methods to
assess the equity and inclusiveness of SDSPs. Leveraging qualitative approaches would likely prove essential to an effective assessment of equity
and inclusion. There are many axes to equity and inclusion, including hiring and promotion practices and accessibility of programming. Establishing
clear definitions and goals for equity and inclusion in and through SDSPs would be a valuable contribution to the field.
To conclude, SDSPs should carefully consider which metrics to collect and design a plan to effectively gather and analyse those collected
metrics. This will provide a firm foundation to deliver on internal and external reporting needs, as well as facilitate program evaluation. This paper
provides ideas for success metrics and describes data sources and potential considerations in gathering such metrics. As SDSPs engage in this
work, they should examine the ethical, privacy and legal implications of their work. Additionally, we hope that further development of this area of
the field will yield new mechanisms to assess our programs beyond just success and provide ways to look at deeper nuances in the provision
of statistical and data science consulting and collaboration.
