Rachel Levy is the executive director of the Data Science Academy and professor of Mathematics at NC State. She was the principal investi
gator on the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation grant that supported this work. Levy is a fellow of the Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics.
Her writing has been featured in the Princeton University Press Best Writing on Mathematics and has been recognized by the NCTM award
for linking research and practice. In addition to writing textbooks, scientific articles and career guides, she has written and blogged for Ameri
can Scientist and founded the internationally recognized blog Grandma got STEM. Her research focuses on mathematical modelling and data
science education.
Micaela Parker is the founder and executive director of the Academic Data Science Alliance (ADSA). ADSA is a professional network for aca
demic data science leaders, practitioners and educators. Through collaborative events and community-driven resource development, ADSA
advances data science and AI best practices in higher education. Before launching ADSA, Micaela worked for the Moore-Sloan Data Science
Environments and was an executive director for the University of Washington's eScience Institute where she developed data science research
and training programs. Based on her years of experience, she has authored several papers offering guidance for emerging data science initia
tives. She was a co-investigator on the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation grant that supported this work.
Marianne Huebner is the director of the Center of Statistical Training and Consulting (CSTAT) and a Professor of the Department of Statistics
and Probability at Michigan State University, East Lansing, Michigan. CSTAT reports to the Office of Research and Innovation and has full
time staff members as well as graduate students research assistants who collaborate with faculty, research staff and graduate students across
the university and provide statistical support to external clients. CSTAT website: https://cstat.msu.edu/.
